{
  "case_id": "2024-24-304",
  "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nNOEL J. FRANCISCO, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nSOPAN JOSHI, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting neither party.\nDEEPAK GUPTA, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Two basic principles resolve this case. First, a class action is just a tool for aggregating claims. So, if an individual plaintiff doesn't have Article III standing to bring his own claim, he can't bring it as part of a class either. That's why Laroe held that an intervenor has to show Article III standing in order to intervene, and, as Justice Scalia said in Shady Grove, class actions are just another species of joinder.\nSecond, Rule 23(b)(3)'s predominance requirement leads to the same result. If a class is defined to include plaintiffs without Article III standing and, as a result, you need thousands of mini-trials to separate the wheat"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0000-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case is a perfect example. Plaintiffs who don't want to use kiosks don't have standing to challenge how kiosks work any more than a vegan has standing to challenge how a restaurant defines a medium rare steak. As a result, the Court needs to assess whether each of the 8,000 to 112,000 class members actually want to use kiosks, and that will necessarily swamp any common issues, as the D.C. and First Circuits correctly held in the Rail Freight and Asacol cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: The -- in this case, there have been a number of orders, and it seems as though the one that we have before us is the May order, which is inoperative. Would you spend a minute on why we still -- we have jurisdiction over the May order when there have been subsequent orders?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FRANCISCO: And the mechanism has\nto be one that protects the defendant's rights.\nAlso, the other thing I would point to\nis that, unlike damages, which come at the end\nof the case --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The problem with that is it walks you straight\ninto the Rule 23(b)(3) issue where the question\nis, when you've got an overly broad class\ndefinition, is there a way that you can seper\nout -- separate out the injured and uninjured\nadministry in -- in an administrable way."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the question only becomes pertinent when you're trying to give a damage award to anyone."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Francisco",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If they lose, if the class is certified and they lose, the entire class is bound by that adverse judgment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So I want to pick up there and also go back to the Chief Justice's question and make sure I understand what you're saying, which is that the Article III question, you're saying, in a case like this is not so hard to solve, that it's -- it's merely a matter of taking it from all the people who walked into"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I am struggling to understand your argument."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Francisco",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under Rule 23(f), we are way past the 14-day period to appeal the August order. So there would have to be then some other -- some kind of equitable tolling concept that gets built into and on top of that. And, as I said, if you were to do that -- and -- and I would strongly urge you not to -- I would be vigorously arguing for anything I could think of to allow us to appeal that August order well past the 14-day period of limitations under 28(f), and I would do my best to succeed. I just can't represent to you what I think the answer is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Francisco",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So another way to look at this would be for you to say, okay, there might be some procedural quirks, maybe they flow from the Ninth Circuit's odd, you know, way of deciding what orders are appealable, what orders are operative, but that, here, you should just decide the question presented on the facts as they've come up to you because you do have a judgment in front of you from the Ninth Circuit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Francisco",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 78,
        "start": 78
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So suppose we have a Verizon customer who brings a class action against the company, arguing that Verizon charged her and all customers certain fees over a six-month time period that she says were unlawful. And this is a claim that does not have an element of harm in it. She's just saying these fees, unlawful, you weren't allowed to do it. And she seeks to certify a class of all Verizon customers during that six-month time frame."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Sopan Joshi",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0003-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 84,
        "start": 84
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court has frequently said that Rule 23 requires all class members to share the same injury. We think that includes an Article III injury. So, if there are members of a class that aren't even injured, they can't share the same injury with the other class members."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So what would you do at the early stages of the litigation, say, post-certification, and -- and you find injured -- uninjured parties in the -- in the class?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 89,
        "start": 89
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "class must be limited to injured persons?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: So an Article III injury is required? It's a backdoor way of getting to Petitioner's position, I think."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 91,
        "start": 91
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The rule requires commonality and predominance. This Court has interpreted those terms in Rule 23 to require all class members to share the same injury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0004-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 107,
        "start": 107
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As an amicus, we're poorly situated to take a strong view of matters, but, that said, I think -- I think Petitioners have the better of the argument, and what I would rely on are two things."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0004-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 107,
        "start": 107
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The August order does not purport to enter a new class or certify a new class and get rid of the old one."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0005-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 112,
        "start": 112
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "there is a strong through line of this Court's class action cases, Falcon, Amchem, Walmart, Lewis against Casey, so many cases, East Texas Motor Freight, in which the Court has said that different injuries --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Joshi",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 113,
        "start": 113
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So there are cases of both types, and Comcast is the perfect example of the case you just said. In Comcast, the plaintiffs had an antitrust theory that was common to the class that was common to the defendants that would have established, you know, the injury, causation, et cetera, but it was the variation in damages that precluded certification of that class because it's just so fundamental to the kind of claim that was being brought that it was just going to overwhelm even the common antitrust liability theory, and we're saying Article III injury is just as fundamental and --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0005-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 115,
        "start": 115
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "of the August order on which LabCorp's arguments have exclusively relied was not actually in the case in the court of appeals and, therefore, isn't within this Court's certiorari jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gupta",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0005-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 116,
        "start": 116
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "brief in opposition did not raise this jurisdictional problem. It did raise the -- the fundamental problem that in -- in the Respondents' view, the question presented is not presented by this case and other preservation issues."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0005-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 119,
        "start": 119
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you think this material modification rule is required by statute?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gupta",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0005-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 122,
        "start": 122
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But I just want to point out we're here, you know, in -- in this case talking about 23(f) appealability jurisprudence in a case where my friends didn't challenge the jurisdictional holding of the court of appeals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0005-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 128,
        "start": 128
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the general rule in this Court's cases -- and this comes up when you have, for example, a preliminary injunction that has been outstripped and then you had an appeal from the preliminary injunction. This Court has said those appeals are moot."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0006-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 132,
        "start": 132
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Well, Mr. Gupta, that -- that's the question that we didn't get to in Acheson, right? There are arguments that racial discrimination and other kinds of discrimination are different."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0006-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 141,
        "start": 141
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Do you think the amicus briefs are wrong then? They're just not understanding their own interest?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0007-0000",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 153,
        "start": 153
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "Class-wide judgments and their finality and their preclusive effect under our current law is predicated on adequate representation and due process. And I think you would be breaking the system if you were to adopt their position that makes Article III a necessary prerequisite and -- and invites collateral attacks and retrospective inquiries into the finality of class judgments."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Noel J. Francisco)",
      "case_id": "2024-24-304",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe7c353a4d6a787d6c64e9a236efb85b94f86b44896533fc721664eea07b2d13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe7c353a4d6a-0007-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
